<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Place des Vosges</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1605">1605</a>–<a href="/years/1612">1612</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Place Royale</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Place des <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> is a square in the Marais district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, built between 1605 and 1612 on the orders of Henry IV and originally called the Place Royale. Surrounded by uniform houses of brick and stone above arcades, it is the oldest planned square in Paris and a model for later royal squares in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It took its present name in 1800.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/place-des-vosges.jpg" alt="Place des Vosges" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Des Racines et des Ailes · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Place_des_Vosges_vue_a%C3%A9rienne.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Place des <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a>, originally the Place Royale, was built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV of France</a> from 1605 to 1612 in the Marais district, on the site of the Hôtel des Tournelles and its gardens, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-de-medici">Catherine de' Medici</a> had demolished. It is a true square of 140 metres by 140 metres and embodied one of the first European programmes of royal city planning, although the Plaza Mayor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, begun in 1590, precedes it. Before the square was completed, Henry IV ordered the Place Dauphine laid out. Within five years the king oversaw additions to the Louvre Palace, the Pont Neuf, the Hôpital Saint Louis and the two royal squares.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Place Royale was inaugurated in 1612 with a grand carrousel to celebrate the engagement of Louis XIII and Anne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. What was new was that the house-fronts were all built to one design, probably by Jean Baptiste Androuet du Cerceau: red brick with strips of stone quoins over vaulted arcades standing on square pillars, under steeply pitched blue slate roofs pierced with small-paned dormers. Only the north range was built with the vaulted ceilings that the galleries were meant to have.</p><p>Two pavilions rise above the unified roofline on the north and south faces and give access to the square through triple arches. They are called the Pavilion of the King and the Pavilion of the Queen, but no royal ever lived in the square except Anne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, who lived for a short while in the Pavilion de la Reine. Cardinal Richelieu had an equestrian bronze of Louis XIII erected in the centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Place Royale became a prototype of the residential squares of later European cities and inspired later developments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. It was a fashionable and expensive place to live in the 17th and 18th centuries and one of the main reasons for the chic of the Marais among the Parisian nobility, who used it as a meeting place until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. Residents included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a> from 1615 to 1627, Sully, Henri IV's minister, the courtesan Marion Delorme from 1639 to 1648, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a> from 1832 to 1848; his house is now a museum.</p><p>In 1800 the square was formally renamed Place des <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a>, in honour of the département of Vosges, the first to pay taxes supporting a campaign of the Revolutionary army. The Restoration monarchs restored the original name from 1814 until 1830 and again from 1852 until 1870, and in 1830 it was briefly the Place de la République. The revolutionary name returned in 1870.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Place des Vosges” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Stanisław Leszczyński the architect Emmanuel Héré and the ironworker Jean Lamour created the <b>Place Royale</b>, now Place Stanislas, in 1752–1755, one of the finest urban ensembles of eighteenth-century Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Charleville was laid out on a regular plan around the Place Ducale, modelled on the <b>Place des Vosges</b> in Paris.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-arches-charleville">Principality of Arches-Charleville</a></li></ul></section>
